     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 4000 mg per dose is well into the range where ibuprofen overdose toxicity is reported, and 16 g/day over a day would likely cause serious harm. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't make that dose safe. Any intended effect is also unlikely to improve at that dose, because ibuprofen's analgesic and anti-inflammatory effect plateaus well below it.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- Enter ibuprofen at a standard dose, such as 400 mg or 800 mg PO, with the frequency you choose. I'll keep it within 3,200 mg/day, or 2,400 mg/day if you prefer the inpatient limit.\n- Enter a different agent or a multimodal regimen if the goal is stronger pain or inflammation control.\n- Add a note to the order if you want to document a specific clinical intent.\n\nIf there's a protocol or clinical reason behind the 4000 mg figure, please send it. It may be a unit or transcription error, and I can help work out the intended regimen. Otherwise, a pharmacist or toxicologist is the right person to consult before anything above the labeled maximum is ordered."
      }
     ],
